<!-- slide-01.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Inside a Grade eleven Chemistry lesson.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    Alkenes — the building blocks of plastics, fuels, and a thousand reactions.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    Watch what Aditi sees on her laptop tonight.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-02.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Meet Aditi — Grade eleven Science, Chemistry stream.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    Tonight she's working through reactions of alkenes.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    Lesson, quiz, tutorial — about twenty-five minutes of focused study.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-03.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    She opens her dashboard.
    <break time="300ms"/>
    Continue where you left off — reactions of alkenes is queued.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    One click. The lesson opens.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-04.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    The lesson page opens.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    Title, the first section, a definition of alkenes —
    pitched two grades below her own so it never feels like a wall of jargon.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-05.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Section two — electrophilic addition.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    The defining reaction class. Hydrogen with a palladium catalyst.
    <break time="200ms"/>
    H Br. Bromine. Water with sulfuric acid.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    All add across the double bond.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-06.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Markovnikov's rule.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    The rich get richer.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    The heavier fragment lands on the more substituted carbon.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    Below — key points she can scan before the quiz.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-07.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Worked example. Propene plus H Br.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    Two possible products — but Markovnikov's rule picks the winner.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    Two-bromopropane. The heavy bromine goes to the carbon with fewer H atoms.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-08.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Quiz time.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    Eight questions. She thinks about A briefly, lands on B.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    Two-bromopropane — the Markovnikov product.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-09.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    Correct.
    <break time="300ms"/>
    The explanation appears below — Markovnikov's rule, in two short sentences.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    Wrong answers don't get a red X. They get the reasoning.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-10.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    A tutorial section opens.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    Step one — identify the double bond and the electrophile.
    <break time="200ms"/>
    Step two — apply Markovnikov.
    <break time="200ms"/>
    Step three — write the new structure.
  </prosody>
</speak>

<!-- slide-11.mp3 -->
<speak>
  <prosody rate="medium">
    She finishes the unit.
    <break time="350ms"/>
    Eight nights of chemistry in a row.
    <break time="400ms"/>
    The streak counter knows — and so does the encouragement nudge,
    but only when it's earned.
  </prosody>
</speak>

